/*
 * TWO WAYS TO BE BROKEN, and the interesting one is the second.
 *
 * BAD_OUTER moves the TBSCertificate's length by one. Every X.509 parser in the
 * world rejects it, which makes it the easy case and worth pinning only so the
 * structural check in the test is known to have teeth.
 *
 * BAD_INNER moves the length of the OCTET STRING that holds the claims set, one
 * level inside the extension. OPENSSL ACCEPTS IT. SO DOES PYTHON
 * `cryptography`. Neither descends into an extension value it does not know,
 * so to both of them the certificate is well formed and the evidence is an
 * opaque blob. That is precisely the failure this project has to fear: a
 * certificate that parses somewhere, is relayed by a gateway that checked it,
 * and means nothing. Only a check that walks the extension's own DER sees it,
 * and so the test walks it.
 */
